{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Root cause — PROVEN by captured request + live A/B, not inferred\n\nThe only `cache_control` breakpoint rebar places is on the SYSTEM-prompt block (plus tool\ndefinitions). At both failing call sites that block is tiny and the real payload rides in the USER\nmessage, AFTER the breakpoint, unmarked. Anthropic silently declines to cache a sub-minimum marked\nprefix: no error, `cache_creation=0`, `cache_read=0`.\n\n`src/rebar/llm/capabilities.py:333-355` — `cache_settings_for` sets only\n`anthropic_cache_instructions` and `anthropic_cache_tool_definitions`; its own docstring states\n\"neither `*_cache_messages` nor `anthropic_cache` is touched\". In pydantic-ai those flags mark only\nthe last system block and the last tool.\n\nCAPTURED OUTBOUND REQUESTS (httpx transport wrapped at the `anthropic_model.py:95-161` seam, real\nconfig/runner/prompts, body dumped before it left the process):\n\n    coach_notes            system[0]  ~150 tok  cache_control={ephemeral,5m}\n                           user msg  ~6928 tok  cache_control=None      tools: 0\n    enrich/ticket-digest   system[0]  ~477 tok  cache_control={ephemeral,5m}\n                           user msg  ~6326 tok  cache_control=None      tools: 0\n\nLIVE A/B isolating prefix SIZE from every other variable — identical marker, identical large user\nmessage, only the marked-prefix size varies:\n\n    haiku-4-5    1193 tok -> w=0    r=0       NOT CACHED\n    haiku-4-5    2748 tok -> w=0    r=0       NOT CACHED\n    haiku-4-5    4749 tok -> w=4742 r=4742    CACHED\n    sonnet-4-5    253 tok -> w=0    r=0       NOT CACHED\n    sonnet-4-5   7930 tok -> w=7923           CACHED\n\nEmpirical floor sits between 2748 and 4749 for BOTH models. rebar marks 150 and 477.\n\nWHY THE PASS-1 FINDERS CACHE FINE in the same process: `plan_review/passes.py` returns\n`shared_plan_prefix(plan) + body` for `PASS_FINDER`, putting a multi-thousand-token prefix in the\nSYSTEM slot — those are the 5781-9385 cache_write rows in the same run.\n\nContributing detail for coach_notes: `reviewers/plan_review_coach.md:20` places `<!--volatile-->` two\nlines from the end, so `resolve_prompt_cached` leaves only ~9 lines of stance prose in the system slot\nand routes `{{plan}}` to the user message.\n\n## Rejected hypotheses\n\n* \"No breakpoint is placed\" — REJECTED: the captured request shows `cache_control` on `system[0]` in\n  both cases.\n* \"The prefix is unstable between calls\" — REJECTED twice: `cache_write=0` means no write was ever\n  ATTEMPTED, and an unstable-but-marked prefix still writes on every call; separately the marked block\n  is byte-stable by construction.\n* \"Provider-side caching unavailable\" — REJECTED: finders cached in the same process, same provider.\n\n## The two sites are NOT the same problem\n\n* `coach_notes` is genuine RECOVERABLE bleed. The plan is ALREADY in the cache, written by the Pass-1\n  finders that carry it in the system prefix. The coach re-sends the same plan in a different slot, so\n  it cannot hit that entry and re-pays full price every run.\n* `enrich`/ticket-digest is different in kind. Its ~6.3k user payload is one ticket's text, unique per\n  call, so even a message-level breakpoint would only ever WRITE, never READ. There is no reusable\n  prefix at that site at all — a naive fix there ADDS cost.\n\n## A separate, sharper defect this exposed in the warning itself\n\n`warn_if_cache_ineffective` (`structured_run.py:417-422`) compares total `input_tokens` against\n`CACHE_MIN_PREFIX_TOKENS`, but what actually governs caching is the MARKED PREFIX size. The two\nwarnings observed were true positives only by coincidence of the totals clearing 4096. The same\npredicate applied to the marked prefix (150 / 477) would have named the cause directly instead of\nmerely flagging a symptom. Note also that 4096 is roughly right for sonnet-4-5 and haiku-4-5 too, so\nthe \"OPUS floor\" comment understates its generality.\n\n## Where the fix belongs\n\nNOT at the call sites. Moving payload back into the system prompt reverses story c6e5's stable-prefix\nsplit, and makes no sense for enrich. `cache_settings_for` is the single place that decides breakpoint\nplacement and explicitly opts out of message-level caching. Two shared-seam options to weigh: mark the\nlast user content block (`anthropic_cache_messages`), or Anthropic's automatic top-level\n`cache_control` (`anthropic_cache`). Either NEEDS A GUARD so a no-reusable-prefix site like enrich does\nnot pay write overhead for a read that never comes.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The fix is made at the `cache_settings_for` seam, not per call site, and is guarded so a site\n      with no reusable prefix (enrich/ticket-digest) does not pay cache-write overhead for a read that\n      never comes.\n- [ ] A test pins that `coach_notes` sends its payload behind a cache breakpoint, and fails before the\n      fix.\n- [ ] A test pins that a no-reusable-prefix site is NOT given a breakpoint it cannot benefit from.\n- [ ] `warn_if_cache_ineffective` measures the MARKED PREFIX rather than total `input_tokens`, so the\n      warning names the cause; a test covers a large-total / small-marked-prefix call.\n- [ ] [operator-attested] a real `rebar review-plan` run after the fix shows nonzero cache_write for\n      the previously-zero `coach_notes` call, cited from REBAR_USAGE_LOG with run sha and timestamp.\n- [ ] make lint and make typecheck pass by exit code; the default selection is green."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785709523494159001,"uuid":"ee76abed-b7fc-48df-83f6-754f91279846"}